  "content": {
    "title": "March Madness 2026: Who won Duke vs. Siena basketball game?",
    "text": "Siena guard Gavin Doty drives to the basket past Duke forward Cameron Boozer during the first half in the first round of the NCAA college basketball tournament, Thursday, March 19, 2026, in Greenville, S.C. (AP Photo/Chris Carlson)Chris Carlson/Associated PressGREENVILLE, S.C. — As the Siena men’s basketball team built an almost unfathomable 13-point lead over Duke in the second half on Thursday afternoon, Bon Secours Wellness Arena began sounding a lot more like MVP Arena in downtown Albany.Of course, the Siena fans in sections 115 and 116 were delirious. But they were also joined in the cheering by the neutral fans who love an underdog or simply those who hate Duke, the Atlantic Coast Conference juggernaut and five-time national champion.Article continues below this ad“At the beginning of the game, it was, 'Let’s go, Duke,'” Siena sophomore guard Gavin Doty said. “And, the next thing you know, the whole arena is shouting, 'Let’s go, Saints.' Just the respect that we got throughout that 40 minutes of that game, Siena’s on the map. If you didn’t know who Siena was, you do now.”Though Siena caught the attention of a nation watching on CBS, the 16th-seeded Saints came up just short of what would have been one of the greatest upsets in the history of the NCAA Tournament. The Blue Devils, the overall No. 1 seed, survived the Saints with a 71-65 victory in a first-round game.Make the Times Union a Preferred Source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceDuke went on a late 15-3 run and took the lead for good at 63-61 on Isaiah Evans' drive with 4:25 left.Article continues below this adDoty scored 21 points, Francis Folefac had 18 points and Brendan Coyle added 12 points for the Metro Atlantic Athletic Conference champion Saints, who finished their season at 23-12.“Obviously, we poured our heart out, but close doesn’t count for much,” said Coyle, a senior forward from Niskayuna.Siena fans Tom Templeton, left, and Dominick Purnomo watch as Siena leads early in the first half of the men’s basketball game between Siena and Duke in the NCAA Division I Men’s Basketball Tournament on Thursday, March 19, 2026, at McGeary's Irish Pub in Albany, N.Y. Siena is the 16th seed and Duke is the number one seed. (Lori Van Buren/Times Union)Lori Van Buren/Times UnionSiena fell short in its bid to become the third No. 16 seed to beat a No. 1 seed, joining UMBC over Virginia in 2018 and Fairleigh Dickinson over Purdue in 2023.Article continues below this ad“I was in the hotel room praying the rosary before the game today and I had full confidence we were going to win this game,” Doty said. “It sucks we came up short, but I am proud of the fight we had.”ACC Player of the Year Cameron Boozer had 22 points and 13 rebounds and his twin brother, Cayden, had 19 points for Duke (33-2), which moves on to play TCU in the second round on Saturday.Saints coach Gerry McNamara nearly played all five starters the full 40 minutes, trying to squeeze out the upset. They appeared to run out of gas. The Saints entered the game with a seven-man rotation because of injuries and the ineligibility of starting forward Antonio Chandler. McNamara opted not to use freshman forward Isaiah Henderson or freshman guard Christian Jones off the bench - except for two seconds that Jones subbed in for Coyle near game's end.“You’re looking at a guy that played 40 minutes a lot,” said McNamara, the former Syracuse star. “We just got here from (the MAAC Tournament in) Atlantic City doing pretty much the same thing.”Article continues below this adMcNamara said he needed the size of graduate center Riley Mulvey and Folefac, the freshman forward, on the court at all times and Coyle, Doty and guard Justice Shoats were too valuable to take out.“The way we looked, the way it felt to me, I went with the group I thought could give us the best chance,” McNamara said. “Doesn’t mean (Henderson and Jones) aren’t great players. They are. They’ve helped us a lot this year.”Justice Shoats #0 of the Siena Saints puts up a shot over Nikolas Khamenia #14 of the Duke Blue Devils during the first round of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament held at Bon Secours Wellness Arena on March 19, 2026 in Greenville, South Carolina. (Photo by Jamie Sabau/NCAA Photos via Getty Images)Jamie Sabau/NCAA Photos via Getty ImagesSiena held a 47-34 lead with 17:26 left before Mulvey and Folefac missed dunks. Duke took advantage by ripping off an 11-0 run to climb within 47-45.Article continues below this adDuke coach Jon Scheyer said McNamara “outcoached” him. Scheyer did adjust by going to a 3-2 zone that helped turn the game in the second half.“G-Mac had his guys way more ready to play than I did,” Scheyer said. “They played at such a high level.”Siena, which shot 54.8% from the field in the first half, made 23.5% in the second half — its worst half of the season.The Blue Devils outrebounded the Saints 30-13 in the second half.Article continues below this ad“That game was ours to take,” Cameron Boozer said. “The first half, we put ourselves in that position. We knew we were going to be able to come out of it.”Cameron Boozer #12 of the Duke Blue Devils defends against Riley Mulvey #55 of the Siena Saints during the first half in the first round of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Bon Secours Wellness Arena on March 19, 2026 in Greenville, South Carolina. (Photo by Jared C. Tilton/Getty Images)Jared C. Tilton/Getty ImagesSiena went on a 10-0 run to seize a 26-16 lead with 10:21 remaining in the first half. Coyle started it with back-to-back 3-pointers.Then, Folefac scored back-to-back baskets, the first off a Doty steal, for the 10-point lead.Article continues below this adSiena continued the pressure with a Folefac fast-break dunk on a pass from Doty and a Mulvey dunk on a follow.Siena held a 43-32 halftime lead, making Duke the first No. 1 seed to trail a No. 16 seed by double digits at halftime in NCAA Tournament history, according to ESPN Research.March 19, 2026|Updated March 20, 2026 9:25 a.m.Mark Singelais covers Siena basketball, UAlbany lacrosse, horse racing and other sports. Contact him at 518-454-5509 or msingelais@timesunion.com.",
